      "summary": "Yazd: The Desert Sanctuary - Cities of Wonder: 40 Most Beautiful Cities in the World, Volume 4",
      "content": "### Chapter 5: Yazd: The Desert Sanctuary\n\n![Yazd - Iconic historic citadel view](https://assets.lingocafe.app/cities-04/images/yazd-character.jpg)\n\nWedged between the northern Dasht-e Kavir and southern Dasht-e Lut deserts, Yazd is one of the oldest continuously inhabited mud-brick cities on Earth. To survive the fierce desert heat, ancient builders engineered a skyline of badgirs (windcatchers)—soaring stone and clay chimneys that catch the slightest desert breeze and funnel cooled air into underground courtyards.\n\n![Geographic location of Yazd in Iran](https://assets.lingocafe.app/cities-04/images/yazd-map.png)\n\nWandering the vaulted adobe alleys of the Fahadan district reveals ancient subterranean qanats (water channels) flowing silently beneath thick clay walls. The city is also the historic heart of Zoroastrianism in Iran, home to the Fire Temple, where a sacred flame has burned continuously for over fifteen hundred years, and the haunting Towers of Silence rising from desert ridges outside the city."
